{"subjectId":"c","id":"conditionals","title":"Conditionals: if, else & ?:","group":"Control Flow & Functions","order":12,"html":"<p class=\"lead\">C has no boolean type in conditions — any scalar value is tested against zero: <code>0</code> (or <code>NULL</code>) is false, everything else is true.</p>\n<section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-arrows-split\"></i>if / else if / else</h3>\n<figure data-lang=\"c\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>C</figcaption><pre><code>#include &lt;stdio.h&gt;\n\nconst char *grade(int score) {\n    if (score &gt;= 90) {\n        return \"A\";\n    } else if (score &gt;= 75) {\n        return \"B\";\n    } else if (score &gt;= 50) {\n        return \"C\";\n    } else {\n        return \"F\";\n    }\n}\n\nint main(void) {\n    int scores[] = {95, 80, 50, 12};\n    for (int i = 0; i &lt; 4; i++)\n        printf(\"%d -&gt; %s\\n\", scores[i], grade(scores[i]));\n    return 0;\n}</code></pre></figure><figure data-lang=\"text\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Output</figcaption><pre><code>95 -&gt; A\n80 -&gt; B\n50 -&gt; C\n12 -&gt; F</code></pre></figure>\n</section><section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-toggle-left\"></i>Truth in C</h3>\n<figure data-lang=\"c\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>C</figcaption><pre><code>#include &lt;stdio.h&gt;\n#include &lt;stdbool.h&gt;\n\nint main(void) {\n    int n = -3;\n    char *p = NULL;\n    if (n) printf(\"-3 is true\\n\");\n    if (!p) printf(\"NULL is false\\n\");\n    if (0.0) printf(\"never\\n\"); else printf(\"0.0 is false\\n\");\n\n    bool done = 5;                  /* any non-zero converts to true (1) */\n    printf(\"done = %d\\n\", done);\n    return 0;\n}</code></pre></figure><figure data-lang=\"text\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Output</figcaption><pre><code>-3 is true\nNULL is false\n0.0 is false\ndone = 1</code></pre></figure>\n</section><section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-question-mark\"></i>The conditional (ternary) operator</h3>\n<figure data-lang=\"c\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>C</figcaption><pre><code>#include &lt;stdio.h&gt;\n\nint main(void) {\n    int a = 7, b = 12;\n    int max = a &gt; b ? a : b;\n    printf(\"max = %d, %s\\n\", max, a % 2 ? \"a is odd\" : \"a is even\");\n    return 0;\n}</code></pre></figure><figure data-lang=\"text\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Output</figcaption><pre><code>max = 12, a is odd</code></pre></figure>\n</section><section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-alert-triangle\"></i>Pitfalls</h3>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Bug</th><th>What happens</th><th>Prevention</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><code>if (x = 5)</code></td><td>Assigns 5, always true</td><td><code>-Wall</code> warns (<code>-Wparentheses</code>); some write <code>if (5 == x)</code></td></tr><tr><td><code>if (x &gt; 0);</code> <code>{ … }</code></td><td>Empty statement — block always runs</td><td>Brace style, <code>-Wempty-body</code></td></tr><tr><td>Dangling <code>else</code></td><td><code>else</code> binds to the <strong>nearest</strong> <code>if</code></td><td>Always use braces</td></tr><tr><td><code>if (0.1 + 0.2 == 0.3)</code></td><td>False — floating rounding</td><td>Compare with a tolerance</td></tr><tr><td><code>if (a &lt; b &lt; c)</code></td><td>Parsed as <code>(a &lt; b) &lt; c</code> → compares 0/1 with <code>c</code></td><td><code>a &lt; b &amp;&amp; b &lt; c</code></td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<figure data-lang=\"c\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>C — rejected: -Wparentheses under -Werror</figcaption><pre><code>#include &lt;stdio.h&gt;\n\nint main(void) {\n    int x = 0;\n    if (x = 5)                 /* assignment used as condition */\n        printf(\"always\\n\");\n    return 0;\n}</code></pre></figure>\n<details class=\"qa\"><summary>What is the dangling else problem?</summary><div><p>In <code>if (a) if (b) s1; else s2;</code> the <code>else</code> belongs to the inner <code>if (b)</code>, regardless of indentation. C resolves the ambiguity by binding <code>else</code> to the nearest unmatched <code>if</code>. Braces make the intent explicit.</p></div></details></section>"}